WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.009
Can computers read our brains and know what
we’re seeing?

00:00:04.009 --> 00:00:06.760
They’re now a small step closer.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:11.460
Researchers at Purdue University are using
artificial neural networks,

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.200
to decode real neural networks.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.289
They scanned three women’s brains as they
watched videos

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:23.619
and recorded brain activity in response to
each clip.

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:30.789
An AI algorithm successfully learned to predict
how their brains would respond to new clips.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:36.730
Another algorithm learned to look at their
brains and predict what they saw.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.520
The reconstructed images looked like fuzzy ghosts.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:47.430
But the AI could at least guess the category
of the content: birds, faces, and so on.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.240
The researchers are using Artificial Intelligence

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.160
to understand the brain,  in order to further improve AI.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.760
Brainy, huh?

